## Welcome to Rubidium™

Rubidium is a *metaverse browser* built on *Sneeze*, the open-source metaverse browser engine. Just as a web browser opens web pages, Rubidium opens *spatial fabrics* — live 3D environments assembled from networked services.

---
**Version 0.3.0** — July 16, 2026

## What's New

- Everything! This is the third release of Rubidium.
- Thank you for being among the first people anywhere to experience the open metaverse.

## New since version 0.2.0

- Several bug fixes for Mac and Linux.
- A first attempt at a GLB file viewer in the inspector. Don't get your hopes up too high.
- Significant updates to the way spatial fabric files are read and the way wasm files interface with the engine.
- Added a Rust crate to provide a robust engine API for wasm programs. More languages coming very soon!
- See tutorials for the new fabric file format. If you made your own fabric prior to this version, you'll need to update. This stuff is still evolving.
- [Spatial Fabric Tutorials](https://omb.wiki/sneeze/examples/index)
- Note that the spatial fabric format will not be considered stable until we reach version 1.0.0

## New since version 0.1.0

- The way lighting is encoded has changed in both the scene and nodes.
- Ambient lighting and spot lighting is now working properly.
- See tutorials for the new format. If you made your own fabric in the last two days, you'll need to update. Sorry about that.
- [Spatial Fabric Tutorials](https://omb.wiki/sneeze/examples/index)

## What you can do today

- Open multiple browser tabs and windows, each with its own independent view
- Peek under the hood with the built-in developer inspector window — press `F12`
- Sign and verify metaverse spatial fabric (.msf) files from the command line (see below)
- Stay current with automatic updates as new versions ship

## Present limitations

Rubidium is at the very beginning of its journey, and this release is an early preview:

- The solar system is a demonstration fabric — it was created for development purposes, but will grow into a fully featured destination as the browser matures
- Discovery is *address-based* for now; *proximity-based* discovery — connecting to what is near you rather than typing an address — is on the roadmap
- There are no in-world labels or heads-up display yet
- Network connectivity is coming very soon — until then, wasm programs run connectionless
- VR and AR (via OpenXR) are coming, but are not ready for use in this build
- Fabric-authoring tools are still taking shape

## Make a spatial fabric of your own

A *metaverse spatial fabric* is a collection of *objects and services* described by a signed .msf file. A fabric you publish and host on your own server can be opened and rendered by any compliant metaverse browser.

Rubidium has built-in command-line tools to sign and verify your .msf files:

- `Rubidium --sign --payload <fabric.json> --key <key.pem> --cert <cert.pem> --out <fabric.msf>`
- `Rubidium --verify <fabric.msf>`

To learn more about .msf file formats and standards, and to publish your first fabric, start here:

- [Sneeze Engine Documentation](https://omb.wiki/sneeze)
- [Spatial Fabric Tutorials](https://omb.wiki/sneeze/examples/index)

